  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The upper level ridging currently overhead eastern \nNew York and western New England is bringing well-above average \ntemperatures and dry conditions this afternoon. There is potential \nfor near-record to record-breaking maximum temperatures today, with \nKPOU already breaking their 1987 & 2015 record of 74 deg F with 75 \ndeg F (as of 0110 PM).\n\nA low over the Great Lakes is moving into E'rn Canada with a weak \ncold front extending to its south and approaching the NE'rn U.S. \nfrom the west. Associated rain ahead of the front is expected to \nmove into the Mohawk Valley and Western Adirondacks just before \nsunrise tomorrow, before overspreading the Capital District by late \nmorning. This event will be short-lived, with showers scattering out \nover eastern New York by the afternoon as the front begin to moves \ninto western New England. Persistent southerly flow will continue to \nadvect moisture from the Gulf and Atlantic into the region, with \nPWATs reaching +3 to +4 STDEV above normal throughout tomorrow. \nHigher rainfall amounts are anticipated in the Adirondacks due to \nconsistent upsloping, with probabilistic NBM guidance showing a 70-\n80% chance of at least 0.5 inches and a 50-60% chance of at least \n0.75 inches. The Capital District can expect less overall, with only \na 20-30% chance of reaching a quarter of an inch. However, this \nrainfall will officially break the longest true dry spell for Albany \nsince 1980. Breezy conditions are also expected to continue on \nThursday, particularly in N/S oriented valley regions which could \nsee gusts upwards of 20-30 mph.\n\nA stronger shortwave rotating along the base of the existing trough \nwill provide better ascent for heavier rainfall on Friday. Continued \nstrong southerly fetch focused along the frontal boundary will \nmaintain strong moisture transport from the western Atlantic, \ncontributing to this rainfall. However, the front is expected to \nstall somewhere over New England due to ridging to the east. Where \nthis front eventually stalls will determine where the highest \nrainfall amounts will be concentrated. At this time, ensembles and \ncluster guidance are showing rain primarily impacting Litchfield \nCounty (within our CWA), with low to medium confidence (20-40%) for \nrainfall amounts above 0.25 inches for that area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Unsettled weather is expected to continue into the \nweekend, with a closed low developing over the Great Lakes region \nwithin the aforementioned trough on Friday and migrating toward the \nMid-Atlantic and Northeast. With distinct diffluence ahead of this \nlow and negative tilting of the trough, it sets our region up for \nadditional rainfall on Saturday. There is still spread within model \nguidance regarding the exact track of this low, which will \nultimately determine the placement of the greatest chances of \nprecipitation and exact amounts. There is also plenty of cold air \nadvection associated with this low, which will start to bring \ntemperatures across eastern New York and western New England closer \nto normal as we head into early next week. Euro ensembles are \nshowing a 50-80% chance of at least 0.10 inches in the period \nbetween 12Z Saturday and 12Z Sunday.",
